% === provenance: HuntThomas1999 ===
% CLAIM: The principle that a program should not repeat itself -- and the
%   name "DRY, Don't Repeat Yourself" -- originates with Hunt and Thomas,
%   The Pragmatic Programmer (1999); the reason given is a maintenance one:
%   when duplicated knowledge changes, every copy must be changed.
% FOUND-VIA: known-item verification, not discovery.  (1) Existence and
%   metadata: curl -s
%   'https://openlibrary.org/api/books?bibkeys=ISBN:020161622X&format=json&jscmd=data'
%   -- returns "The Pragmatic Programmer: From Journeyman to Master", Andy
%   Hunt / Dave Thomas, 321 pp., LCCN 99043581, OL16958649M.  (2) Crosscheck:
%   curl -s 'https://api.openalex.org/works?filter=title.search:pragmatic%20programmer'
%   -- W1596790865, 1999, no DOI (trade book, so no Crossref record; hence no
%   doi field on this entry).  (3) Wording of the principle: WebFetch of the
%   PUBLISHER'S OWN excerpt https://media.pragprog.com/titles/tpp20/dry.pdf
%   (the topic "The Evils of Duplication" from the 20th Anniversary Edition),
%   then pdftotext -layout locally.  (4) 1999-edition wording and Tip number:
%   WebFetch https://flylib.com/books/en/1.315.1.22/1/ (third-party
%   reproduction of the 1999 text -- see VERIFIED for how this is weighted).
%   Preceding WebSearch: "Every piece of knowledge must have a single,
%   unambiguous, authoritative representation within a system" Pragmatic
%   Programmer Hunt Thomas -- used only to locate candidate full-text
%   sources, never as evidence.
% FOUND-VIA (here): backed in *Algoritmiskt tänkande* (modules/
%   computational-thinking/slides), bilaga E; entry and provenance copied
%   verbatim from that deck's ltnotes.bib. No search is repeated here.
% PICKED: the book itself is the origin, so no alternatives compete for the
%   attribution.  Cited as the 1999 first edition (Hunt & Thomas) rather than
%   the 2019 20th Anniversary Edition (Thomas & Hunt) because the claim is
%   about where DRY comes from; the 2019 edition explicitly refers back to
%   the first edition for the principle ("In the first edition of this book
%   we did a poor job of explaining just what we meant by Don't Repeat
%   Yourself"), which confirms the 1999 edition as the origin.
% QUOTE (topic "The Evils of Duplication"; Tip 11 in 1999 / Tip 15 in 2019):
%   "We feel that the only way to develop software
%   reliably, and to make our developments easier to understand and maintain,
%   is to follow what we call the DRY principle: Every piece of knowledge
%   must have a single, unambiguous, authoritative representation within a
%   system."  And, for the maintenance reason the module gives: "The
%   alternative is to have the same thing expressed in two or more places. If
%   you change one, you have to remember to change the others, or, like the
%   alien computers, your program will be brought to its knees by a
%   contradiction. It isn't a question of whether you'll remember: it's a
%   question of when you'll forget."
% VERIFIED: full text of the publisher's own PDF excerpt read locally (both
%   quoted passages appear there verbatim); book metadata confirmed against
%   OpenLibrary and OpenAlex.  Honest limitation: the copy carrying the
%   quotes is the 2019 20th Anniversary Edition, not a scan of the 1999
%   printing.  The 1999 wording, and its Tip number 11, were checked against
%   a third-party reproduction (flylib) which returns the same two sentences
%   verbatim.  I did NOT obtain the 1999 printing itself, so no 1999 page
%   number is asserted here -- cite the topic name, not a page.  Applies
%   because the quote states the principle, gives it the name DRY, and gives
%   exactly the module's maintenance reason (change one, you must remember to
%   change the others).
% VERIFIED (here): not re-read; this deck makes the same two claims that
%   bilaga E backs (the principle is Hunt and Thomas's, and the reason is a
%   maintenance one) and adds no new use of the source.
% COUNTER: scholar search "DRY don't repeat yourself principle software"
%   -n dry-principle -p dblp -p openalex -p scopus -p ieee --limit 25 --
%   surfaced no work attributing DRY to an earlier author or disputing the
%   attribution; the closest items ("Staying DRY with OO and FP", 2024;
%   "When Do You Repeat Yourself? Voices from the Trenches of Linux Kernel
%   Maintainers on Code Duplication", 2026) both treat Hunt & Thomas as the
%   source.  No refuting work found for the attribution.
% DATE: 2026-09-02
@book{HuntThomas1999,
  title = {The Pragmatic Programmer: From Journeyman to Master},
  author = {Hunt, Andrew and Thomas, David},
  year = {1999},
  publisher = {Addison-Wesley},
